+---
+id: task-003-make-applypatch-conform-to-rfc-6902-in-every-case-errors-included
+type: task
+title: "Make applyPatch conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Make applyPatch conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some
+edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the way they
+read the standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902
+in every case, errors included. It follows task-002.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- An invalid patch is rejected as an invalid patch before any operation is applied, so that a later
+  invalid entry is not reported as a failed operation of an earlier one.
+- Every operation of the patch is checked, including holes in a sparse patch array, which must not be
+  skipped.
+- `value` of `add`, `replace` and `test` must be a JSON value: `undefined`, non-finite numbers, bigints,
+  functions, symbols, non-plain objects (Date, Map, class instances) and cyclic values are rejected.
+- The input document must be a JSON value too, so that a non-JSON document is not silently cloned.
+- `move` from a location to itself, including the whole document to itself, is a no-op when the
+  `from` location exists (RFC 6902 section 4.4: the `from` location must not be a proper prefix of
+  `path`, and an identical location is not a proper prefix).
+- The failure behaviour is unchanged: a failing patch throws and leaves the input unmodified.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- Tests cover each item above, and the existing tests keep passing with `node --test`.
+- No runtime dependency is added; `src/index.ts` keeps its public types.
+
+## Notes
+
+- Reuse `parsePointer` from task-001.
+
+## Outcome
+
+Gaps found by probing the task-002 implementation, and what changed in `src/index.ts`:
+
+- Non-JSON values were accepted as `value` of `add`, `replace` and `test` (`undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity`,
+  `Date`, `Map`, bigints, cyclic objects) and as document members. They are now rejected, by
+  `isJsonValue`, as an invalid patch or an invalid document.
+- A hole in the patch array was skipped by `forEach`. It is now an invalid operation.
+- The patch was checked one operation at a time, so an invalid operation after a failing one was reported
+  as the failure. The whole patch is now checked before the first operation is applied.
+- `move` from a location to the same location, including `""` to `""`, threw. It is now a no-op once the
+  `from` location is shown to exist. A `move` into a child of its `from` still throws.
+
+`remove` of the whole document still throws: RFC 6902 does not define a document with no value.
+
+Tests: `npm test` runs 50 tests, all passing. No TypeScript compiler is installed in this environment, so
+the strict type check of `tsconfig.json` was not run.
